course: dictionary
lesson: 1159
title: The edge of something
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word brink means the edge of something, such as a steep cliff or water. It also means the point just before something very good or very bad happens. Look at the words around it: «on the brink of war» tells you the war has not started yet, but it is very close.
explain: Read the context: «Conservation means protecting living things and their homes so that animals on the edge can come back, and it works: bald eagles, humpback whales, red kites and Arabian oryx have all returned from the brink.» These animals were almost gone, so brink means the point of disappearing for ever, not a cliff edge.
explain: Test your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «Animals on the brink of extinction» means animals almost gone. «Pull back from the brink» means stopping just before something terrible happens. If your meaning still makes sense, you have probably got it right.
fact: Brink means the edge of a cliff, a steep place or water.
fact: Brink can also mean the point where something very good or very bad is about to happen.
fact: «On the brink of war» means very close to war, but not yet at war.
fact: «On the brink of extinction» means so nearly gone that it might disappear for ever.
fact: «Pull back from the brink» means stopping just before something terrible happens.
exercise: What does «on the brink of war» mean? | Winning a war easily | Bored of fighting | Lost in a faraway land | *Very close to starting a war
exercise: «Bald eagles have all returned from the brink.» What does this suggest? | They travelled back from the sea | They grew very large | *They came back after nearly disappearing | They left home for a long holiday
exercise: Which word means the edge of a cliff? | bring | *brink | blink | bride
test: «Scientists are on the brink of a breakthrough.» What does brink mean here? | Bored of the experiment | Lost in a dark cave | *Just about to make an important discovery | Far away from any laboratory
test: Leo says brink means a drink of water. Why is he wrong? | *Brink means an edge, or a point just before something big happens | Brink is a kind of fish | Brink means a loud noise | Brink is only used in cooking
exam: Which sentence uses brink to mean the point just before something big happens? | The brick wall was painted red | The brink was very loud | She drank a brink of juice | *The town stood on the brink of flooding
